AB  - Generative AI feedback (evaluative and instructional information produced by large language models in response to student submissions) has become increasingly common in higher education, yet longitudinal evidence on how students’ perceptions of such feedback are associated with academic outcomes remains limited. This three-wave study examined the differential and interactive associations of evaluative and instructional generative AI feedback perception with academic achievement among 6030 Chinese first-year undergraduates, assessed at three time points across consecutive semesters. Cross-lagged panel models showed that evaluative feedback perception was positively associated with subsequent achievement (β =.31 and β =.28, p < .001), whereas instructional feedback perception showed non-significant direct associations. Random intercept cross-lagged panel models confirmed these within-person patterns. The evaluative feedback–achievement association was partially statistically mediated by perceived social support (39.4% of total association), while instructional feedback was fully mediated through this pathway (68.3%). Emotional intelligence moderated these associations in a compensatory pattern, with stronger associations among students with lower emotional intelligence. The evaluative × instructional feedback interaction was positively associated with achievement at the second time point (β =.150, p = .018), with instructional feedback showing significant associations only under high evaluative feedback conditions. This interaction varied across disciplines. These findings extend feedback theory to generative AI contexts and offer preliminary recommendations for integrated AI feedback design.
