AB  - The rapid integration of generative AI (GAI) into management education raises questions about its impact on strategic problem-solving, yet little research has examined how structured GAI interaction supports core managerial competences. This study examines prompt design as an externalisation of strategic reasoning when GAI functions as a cognitive tool rather than a solution generator. It involved 373 MBA students at a fully online Spanish university completing an open-ended prompt design activity in a Business Strategy course, guided by the CRETA-R framework. A quantitative, correlational, cross-sectional design examined relationships between prompt design performance, examination results, and course grades, and compared instructor-based and AI-based evaluations using non-parametric analyses. Prompt design performance was significantly associated with independent examination performance, supporting prompt design as an observable manifestation of strategic problem-solving competence; its association with course grade was weaker once criterion overlap with the CRETA-R task was accounted for. Instructor-based and AI-based evaluations showed no significant mean differences, although the markedly reduced variability of AI-generated scores indicates limited sensitivity in automated assessment, cautioning against reading this as evaluative equivalence between human and AI judgement. These findings position prompt design as a higher-order cognitive meta-skill and provide empirical support for structured, AI-mediated tasks in MBA programmes.
